<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brahmins</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Brahmin, Brahmanical</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Brahmins are the priestly and learned class at the top of the traditional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> social hierarchy, responsible for religious ritual and the transmission of sacred knowledge. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a> they received land grants and royal patronage. Brahmanical norms shaped Gupta law and society, including the legal texts compiled in the period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/brahmins.jpg" alt="Brahmins" width="320" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Internet Archive Book Images · No restrictions · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Group_of_Brahmins_1913.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
